INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt", "verifiedAt", "authorName")
VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'global-hiring-trends-november-2026-20261121', 'news', 'Global',
'Global Hiring Trends November 2026: US Rebounds, UK Vacancies Fall',
'विश्वव्यापी नियुक्ति प्रवृत्ति नोभेम्बर २०२६: अमेरिकामा नियुक्ति बढ्यो, बेलायतमा रिक्ति घट्यो',
'Global hiring trends for November 2026: US payrolls rose 165,000, UK vacancies fell to a 12-year low, and Australia jobless rate hit a five-year high.',
'नोभेम्बर २०२६ को विश्वव्यापी नियुक्ति प्रवृत्ति: अमेरिकामा १,६५,००० रोजगारी थपियो, बेलायतमा रिक्ति १२ वर्षकै न्यून भयो, अस्ट्रेलियामा बेरोजगारी पाँच वर्षकै उच्च भयो।',
$$<p>Global hiring trends in October 2026 point in different directions: the United States added 165,000 jobs and its unemployment rate eased to 4.1%, while Australia's jobless rate rose to a five-year high of 4.9% and the United Kingdom's vacancies fell to their lowest level outside the pandemic since 2014. This report brings together the official October labour-market releases from the United States, the United Kingdom, Canada, Australia and Germany, plus the ManpowerGroup survey of what employers plan to do next.</p><h2>Global hiring trends in October 2026 at a glance</h2><table><tbody><tr><td>Reference month</td><td>October 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Countries covered</td><td>United States, United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, Germany</td></tr><tr><td>Employers surveyed</td><td>39,878 across 42 countries (ManpowerGroup, Q4 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>Next data</td><td>4 December 2026 (United States and Canada November reports)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Five countries at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Unemployment rate</th><th>Change on the month</th><th>Openings / vacancies</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>United States</td><td>4.1%</td><td>-0.1 points</td><td>7.5 million openings (September)</td></tr><tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>4.6%</td><td>-0.3 points</td><td>690,000 vacancies</td></tr><tr><td>Canada</td><td>6.7%</td><td>+0.2 points</td><td>Not reported in this release</td></tr><tr><td>Australia</td><td>4.9%</td><td>+0.1 points</td><td>Not reported in this release</td></tr><tr><td>Germany</td><td>6.3% (registered)</td><td>-0.1 points</td><td>654,000 vacancies</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Unemployment rates use different national definitions and are not directly comparable across countries. Germany's registered rate of 6.3% is not the same measure as its survey-based rate of 4.1% in September. See "How this report was put together" below.</p><h2>United States: hiring picked up, but part-time work rose</h2><p>Employers added 165,000 jobs in October, well above the average monthly gain of 48,000 over the previous 12 months, the <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/empsit.pdf?_sp=5264d057-80b8-4fcc-8cad-a7a77813df00">US Bureau of Labor Statistics</a> reported on 6 November 2026. August and September were both revised up, by 53,000 and 31,000, so the two months together added 84,000 more jobs than first reported. The unemployment rate eased to 4.1% from 4.2% in September, with 7.1 million people unemployed, and has stayed in a narrow range of 4.1% to 4.3% since March.</p><p>The gains were concentrated in three sectors: professional and business services added 70,000 jobs, health care added 42,000, and transportation and warehousing added 25,000, including 17,000 in couriers and messengers. Manufacturing added 19,000 and has now added 101,000 jobs since a low in December 2025. Average hourly earnings reached $38.00, up 0.4% on the month and 3.1% on the year, and the average working week stayed at 34.4 hours.</p><p>The weaker signal sits underneath the headline. The number of people working part time because they could not find full-time work rose by 339,000 to 4.8 million, and 2.0 million people — 27.6% of all unemployed people — had been out of work for 27 weeks or more. Teenage unemployment was 13.9%.</p><p>Demand for workers is still strong. The Bureau's Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey counted 7.5 million openings at the end of September 2026, with 5.4 million hires and 3.4 million quits in the month. That is roughly one opening for every unemployed worker in September — Growentix's own arithmetic on the Bureau's two releases. The next monthly jobs report, covering November, is due on 4 December 2026. See <a href="https://growentix.cloud/usa">USA jobs</a> for current American vacancies.</p><h2>United Kingdom: unemployment fell, but vacancies hit a 12-year low</h2><p>The UK unemployment rate fell to 4.6% in July to September 2026, down 0.3 points on the quarter and on the year, the <a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/bulletins/uklabourmarket/november2026">Office for National Statistics</a> reported on 17 November 2026. The employment rate rose to 75.4%. But the ONS itself warns that the quarterly move partly reflects the rotation of its survey sample — the group leaving the survey had lower employment and higher unemployment than the group replacing it — so the fall should not be read as a clean recovery.</p><p>The other indicators point the other way. Payrolled employees fell by 34,000 in October to 30.2 million, on provisional figures, and vacancies fell by 22,000 to 690,000 in August to October, the lowest level outside the pandemic since July to September 2014. Vacancies fell in 11 of the 18 industry sectors, and the fall was concentrated in the smallest businesses: firms with 1 to 9 employees cut 22,000 vacancies to 73,000, with the ONS reporting that smaller firms say labour costs are affecting their hiring decisions. Youth unemployment (16 to 24) stood at 15.1%. Regular pay grew 3.5% on the year, down from 3.7%, and just 0.2% in real terms. See <a href="https://growentix.cloud/uk">UK jobs</a> and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/uk-nhs-jobs-visa-sponsorship-november-2026-20261121">UK NHS jobs with visa sponsorship</a> for current British openings.</p><h2>Canada: unemployment rose for a second month</h2><p>Canada's unemployment rate rose 0.2 points to 6.7% in October, <a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/261106/dq261106a-eng.htm">Statistics Canada</a> reported on 6 November 2026. It was the second consecutive monthly rise, although the rate is still 0.2 points lower than a year earlier and below the recent peak of 6.9% in April 2026. Employment was little changed, down 4,700, after falls of 68,000 in September and 42,000 in August — three months of job losses in a row. The employment rate fell 0.1 points to 60.5%.</p><p>Young people are feeling it most: unemployment for 15- to 24-year-olds rose 0.7 points to 13.7%. Ontario's rate rose to 7.3% and Alberta's to 7.3%, up 0.9 points in a single month. The November survey, covering the week of 8 to 14 November, is due on 4 December 2026. See <a href="https://growentix.cloud/canada">Canada jobs</a> for current Canadian vacancies.</p><h2>Australia: unemployment hit a five-year high</h2><p>Australia's unemployment rate rose to 4.9% in October 2026, its highest since October 2021, the <a href="https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/labour/employment-and-unemployment/labour-force-australia/latest-release">Australian Bureau of Statistics</a> reported on 19 November 2026. Employment fell by 43,500 in the month — against economists' expectations of a 25,000 gain — and the number of unemployed people rose by 17,800 to 753,700. Youth unemployment (15 to 24) rose to 11.3%, also its highest since October 2021. The participation rate slipped to 66.6%.</p><p>The rise has been fast: the rate was 4.5% in July and has now risen for three months in a row. The Reserve Bank of Australia had expected unemployment to average 4.7% in the December quarter and reach 4.9% only at the end of 2027, according to its November Statement on Monetary Policy, so the October figure arrives more than a year early. The figures come ahead of the Reserve Bank's last meeting of 2026 in December, with the cash rate at 4.85% after five rises this year. See <a href="https://growentix.cloud/australia">Australia jobs</a> for current Australian vacancies.</p><h2>Germany: autumn hiring was weak</h2><p>Registered unemployment in Germany fell by 40,000 to 2.955 million in October, the Federal Employment Agency reported on 30 October 2026, and the registered rate slipped 0.1 points to 6.3%. But after seasonal adjustment, unemployment actually rose by 4,000 to 3.016 million, and the agency's chief, Andrea Nahles, called the autumn decline comparatively weak, saying economic conditions are still not strong enough for an improvement in the labour market. There were 44,000 more unemployed people than a year earlier.</p><p>Registered vacancies stood at 654,000, up 31,000 on the year, and the agency's vacancy index fell one point to 102. Unemployment was highest in Bremen (11.0%) and lowest in Bavaria (4.0%). Germany's survey-based unemployment rate, published by the Federal Statistical Office on the International Labour Organization definition, was 4.1% in September — a different measure from the registered rate, and the two should not be compared directly. Germany's economy grew 0.2% in the third quarter of 2026. See <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/germany-skilled-worker-jobs-2026-20261121">Germany skilled worker jobs</a> for current German openings.</p><h2>What employers say they will do next</h2><p>Employers around the world still plan to hire more than they plan to cut, but the optimism is measured and it is old news. <a href="https://www.manpowergroup.com/-/media/project/manpowergroup/mpg-marketing/pdf/insights/2026/mpg--meos-q4-2026-global--report.pdf">ManpowerGroup's Employment Outlook Survey for Q4 2026</a>, published on 8 September 2026, reports a global Net Employment Outlook of 29% — 43% of employers plan to increase staff between October and December, 14% expect reductions and 41% expect no change. The figure is up 2 points on the quarter and 6 points on the year. The survey interviewed 39,878 employers in 42 countries between 1 and 31 July 2026, so it records what employers said in July, before whatever happened in August and September.</p><p>The strongest hiring intentions are in Asia Pacific and the Middle East, at 33%, with India the strongest of all 42 countries at 54% — 65% of Indian employers plan to add staff. The United Arab Emirates stands at 41%. Singapore is cooler at 13%, unchanged on the quarter and down 7 points on the year. Information is the strongest sector at 42%, followed by tech and IT services at 41% and finance and insurance at 40%. Among employers adding staff, 62% say changing roles and skills are the main driver, and 45% are adding entry-level talent, against 20% pulling back. The next survey, covering the first quarter of 2027, is due in December 2026. The ManpowerGroup figures are employer intentions, not hires — a forecast, not a count.</p><h2>Remote work: scarce postings, heavy demand</h2><p>Remote jobs remain a small share of postings and a large share of applications. <a href="https://vamasters.com/remote-work-statistics-2026/">LinkedIn Economic Graph</a> data for March 2026, reported in April 2026, show 9% of paid US postings were fully remote and 11% hybrid — one in five postings offering some flexibility. Those fully remote jobs drew 37% of all applications, down from 41% a year earlier, and an average remote posting received roughly six times as many applications as a posting that was not remote.</p><p><a href="https://vamasters.com/remote-work-statistics-2026/">Indeed Hiring Lab's Remote Tracker</a> counts differently — keyword mentions across all postings, not labelled remote roles — and put remote or hybrid mentions at 8.7% of US postings at the end of October 2026, against about 3% in February 2020. The UK (15.9%) and Ireland (16.2%) run about twice as high. The two measures are not interchangeable, and neither counts the same thing as the US Bureau of Labor Statistics, which found 21.9% of US workers teleworked in September 2026. See <a href="https://growentix.cloud/remote">remote jobs</a> for current remote openings.</p><h2>Visa rules that changed in November</h2><p>The biggest visa change for jobseekers this month is the US H-1B fee — and the fact that it is not currently being collected. A presidential proclamation requires a $100,000 payment for many new H-1B petitions for workers outside the United States. On 18 September 2026 the proclamation was extended for another 12 months, through 21 September 2027, <a href="https://www.mintz.com/insights-center/viewpoints/2806/2026-09-23-presidential-proclamation-extends-100k-h-1b-fee">Mintz reported</a>. But two federal courts have vacated the agency guidance that implements the payment — in Massachusetts on 8 June 2026 and in California on 30 September 2026 — and the government is appealing, so the fee is not enforceable while the court orders stand. A separate Department of Homeland Security proposal for a $103,265 fee on cap-subject H-1B petitions, published on 25 August 2026, is only a proposal: public comments closed on 24 September 2026 and it is not being charged. Extensions, amendments and changes of status inside the United States are not subject to the $100,000 payment, and the employer, not the worker, pays any petition fee. Read the full picture in <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/h-1b-100000-fee-blocked-november-2026-20261121">H-1B $100,000 fee blocked</a>. For other routes, see the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/singapore-work-visa-for-indians-employment-pass-s-pass-20261120">Singapore work visa guide</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/japan-work-visa-ssw-specified-skilled-worker-indians-20261120">Japan SSW work visa guide</a>.</p><h2>What this means for jobseekers</h2><p>The United States is the strongest of the five markets on the official numbers: the most openings relative to jobseekers, with hiring spread across professional services, health care and transport. Health care added 42,000 jobs in October alone, and the sector has been the steadiest source of new roles all year. In the United Kingdom, vacancies are at a 12-year low outside the pandemic and small firms are pulling back first, so applying broadly matters more than picking a single employer. Canada's weakness is broad rather than local — three months of job losses and youth unemployment at 13.7% — while Australia's rise has been fast enough to arrive a year ahead of the central bank's own forecast. Germany's skilled-worker route remains open even as its overall market stays weak, and employer intentions are strongest in India and the Gulf, where ManpowerGroup records the highest hiring outlooks in the world.</p><h2>What the sources do not say</h2><p>No November data exists yet: the United States and Canada publish their November reports on 4 December 2026, the United Kingdom's next release is on 15 December, and Australia's next release comes in mid-December. This report carries no official October figures for Japan, the UAE or Singapore — only the ManpowerGroup hiring intentions for those markets. It carries no wage figures for Canada, Australia or Germany in this round, because those releases did not publish them. The ManpowerGroup survey records what employers said in July 2026, not what they will actually hire in December. And the H-1B court position may change: the government is appealing both vacatur orders, so the fee question is not settled.</p><h2>How this report was put together</h2><p>Every figure in this report comes from the official release named beside it, checked on 19 November 2026. All figures are seasonally adjusted unless stated otherwise. Unemployment rates are not directly comparable across countries: the United States, United Kingdom, Canada and Australia use household labour-force surveys, while Germany's headline rate counts people registered as unemployed with the Federal Employment Agency — its survey-based rate is a different number. The comparison table and the statement that there is roughly one US opening per unemployed worker are Growentix's own arithmetic on the cited releases. Estimates and forecasts are labelled as such: the ManpowerGroup figures are employer intentions, and the Reserve Bank of Australia figures are forecasts. Nothing in this report is estimated by Growentix without saying so.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/empsit.pdf?_sp=5264d057-80b8-4fcc-8cad-a7a77813df00">US Bureau of Labor Statistics, The Employment Situation — October 2026</a> (released 6 November 2026) and <a href="https://www.bls.gov/news.Release/jolts.htm">Job Openings and Labor Turnover — September 2026</a> (released 3 November 2026); <a href="https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/bulletins/uklabourmarket/november2026">Office for National Statistics, Labour market overview, UK: November 2026</a> (released 17 November 2026); <a href="https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/261106/dq261106a-eng.htm">Statistics Canada, Labour Force Survey, October 2026</a> (released 6 November 2026); <a href="https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/labour/employment-and-unemployment/labour-force-australia/latest-release">Australian Bureau of Statistics, Labour Force, Australia, October 2026</a> (released 19 November 2026); Federal Employment Agency labour-market figures for October 2026 (released 30 October 2026), as reported by <a href="https://www.staffingindustry.com/news/global-daily-news/germanys-temp-staffing-sector-shrinks-21-in-august">Staffing Industry Analysts</a>; <a href="https://www.manpowergroup.com/-/media/project/manpowergroup/mpg-marketing/pdf/insights/2026/mpg--meos-q4-2026-global--report.pdf">ManpowerGroup Employment Outlook Survey, Q4 2026</a> (published 8 September 2026; interviews 1–31 July 2026) and <a href="https://www.manpower.com.sg/en/insights/blogs/2026/09/singapore-employers-take-a-more-selective-approach-to-hiring-as-more-plan-to-hold-headcount-steady">ManpowerGroup Singapore, Q4 2026</a>; <a href="https://vamasters.com/remote-work-statistics-2026/">LinkedIn Economic Graph, US Monthly Economic Insights, April 2026</a> (March 2026 data) and Indeed Hiring Lab Remote Tracker data to 31 October 2026; <a href="https://www.mintz.com/insights-center/viewpoints/2806/2026-09-23-presidential-proclamation-extends-100k-h-1b-fee">Mintz, Presidential Proclamation Extends $100K H-1B Fee</a> (23 September 2026).</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Which of the five countries has the lowest unemployment rate?</h3><p>The United States, at 4.1% in October 2026, according to the Bureau of Labor Statistics. Germany's survey-based rate was 4.1% in September, but that is a different measure from its registered rate of 6.3%, so the two should not be compared directly.</p><h3>Is the United States hiring right now?</h3><p>Yes. Employers added 165,000 jobs in October 2026, and there were 7.5 million openings at the end of September, roughly one for every unemployed worker. At the same time, the number of people working part time because they could not find full-time work rose by 339,000 to 4.8 million.</p><h3>What does the ManpowerGroup survey say about hiring in 2027?</h3><p>Nothing yet. The survey published in September 2026 covers hiring intentions for October to December 2026 only, based on interviews in July 2026. The next survey, covering the first quarter of 2027, is due in December 2026.</p>$$,
$$<p>अक्टोबर २०२६ मा विश्वव्यापी नियुक्ति प्रवृत्ति फरक-फरक दिशामा देखियो: अमेरिकामा १,६५,००० रोजगारी थपियो र बेरोजगारी दर ४.१% मा झर्यो, तर अस्ट्रेलियामा बेरोजगारी पाँच वर्षकै उच्च ४.९% पुग्यो र बेलायतमा रिक्ति महामारीपछिको अवधिमा सन् २०१४ पछिकै न्यून भयो। यो प्रतिवेदनले अमेरिका, बेलायत, क्यानडा, अस्ट्रेलिया र जर्मनीका आधिकारिक अक्टोबर श्रम बजार तथ्यांक र रोजगारदाताले अब के गर्ने भन्ने ManpowerGroup सर्वेक्षणलाई एकै ठाउँमा राखेको छ।</p><h2>अक्टोबर २०२६ एक नजरमा</h2><table><tbody><tr><td>सन्दर्भ महिना</td><td>अक्टोबर २०२६</td></tr><tr><td>समेटिएका देश</td><td>अमेरिका, बेलायत, क्यानडा, अस्ट्रेलिया, जर्मनी</td></tr><tr><td>सर्वेक्षणमा रोजगारदाता</td><td>४२ देशका ३९,८७८ (ManpowerGroup, Q4 २०२६)</td></tr><tr><td>अर्को तथ्यांक</td><td>४ डिसेम्बर २०२६ (अमेरिका र क्यानडाका नोभेम्बर प्रतिवेदन)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>पाँच देश एक नजरमा</h2><table><thead><tr><th>देश</th><th>बेरोजगारी दर</th><th>महिनाको परिवर्तन</th><th>खुला पद / रिक्ति</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>अमेरिका</td><td>४.१%</td><td>-०.१ अंक</td><td>७५ लाख खुला पद (सेप्टेम्बर)</td></tr><tr><td>बेलायत</td><td>४.६%</td><td>-०.३ अंक</td><td>६,९०,००० रिक्ति</td></tr><tr><td>क्यानडा</td><td>६.७%</td><td>+०.२ अंक</td><td>यस प्रतिवेदनमा छैन</td></tr><tr><td>अस्ट्रेलिया</td><td>४.९%</td><td>+०.१ अंक</td><td>यस प्रतिवेदनमा छैन</td></tr><tr><td>जर्मनी</td><td>६.३% (दर्ता गरिएको)</td><td>-०.१ अंक</td><td>६,५४,००० रिक्ति</td></tr></tbody></table><p>बेरोजगारी दर हरेक देशमा फरक तरिकाले नापिन्छ, त्यसैले देशबीच सोझै तुलना गर्न मिल्दैन। जर्मनीको दर्ता गरिएको दर ६.३% हो भने सर्वेक्षणमा आधारित दर सेप्टेम्बरमा ४.१% थियो।</p><h2>अमेरिका: नियुक्ति बढ्यो, तर अंशकालीन काम पनि बढ्यो</h2><p>अक्टोबरमा रोजगारदाताले १,६५,००० रोजगारी थपे, जुन पछिल्लो १२ महिनाको औसत मासिक वृद्धि ४८,००० भन्दा धेरै हो। बेरोजगारी दर ४.२% बाट ४.१% मा झर्यो, र ७१ लाख मानिस बेरोजगार छन्। व्यावसायिक सेवा क्षेत्रमा ७०,०००, स्वास्थ्य सेवामा ४२,००० र यातायात तथा भण्डारणमा २५,००० रोजगारी थपिए। औसत प्रतिघण्टा तलब ३८ डलर पुग्यो, जुन एक वर्षअघिको तुलनामा ३.१% बढी हो। तर पूर्णकालीन काम नपाएर अंशकालीन काम गर्नेको संख्या ३,३९,००० ले बढेर ४८ लाख पुग्यो, र २७ हप्ताभन्दा बढीदेखि बेरोजगार रहेका २० लाख मानिस छन्। सेप्टेम्बर अन्त्यमा ७५ लाख खुला पद थिए — बेरोजगार प्रतिव्यक्ति लगभग एउटा खुला पद।</p><h2>बेलायत: बेरोजगारी घट्यो, तर रिक्ति १२ वर्षकै न्यून</h2><p>जुलाई–सेप्टेम्बर २०२६ मा बेरोजगारी दर ४.६% मा झर्यो, तर तथ्यांक कार्यालय आफैंले चेतावनी दिएको छ कि यो गिरावट आंशिक रूपमा सर्वेक्षण नमुना परिवर्तनको असर हो। तलबमा रहेका कर्मचारी अक्टोबरमा ३४,००० ले घटेर ३ करोड २ लाख भए, र रिक्ति २२,००० ले घटेर ६,९०,००० भयो — महामारीको समयबाहेक सन् २०१४ पछिकै न्यून। १६–२४ वर्ष उमेर समूहमा बेरोजगारी १५.१% छ। नियमित तलब वृद्धि ३.५% मा झर्यो।</p><h2>क्यानडा: बेरोजगारी दोस्रो महिना पनि बढ्यो</h2><p>क्यानडाको बेरोजगारी दर अक्टोबरमा ६.७% पुग्यो, जुन लगातार दोस्रो महिनाको वृद्धि हो। रोजगारी ४,७०० ले घट्यो — अगस्टमा ४२,००० र सेप्टेम्बरमा ६८,००० ले घटेपछि लगातार तीन महिना रोजगारी घटेको छ। १५–२४ वर्ष उमेर समूहमा बेरोजगारी १३.७% पुग्यो। ओन्टारियो र अल्बर्टामा दर ७.३% छ।</p><h2>अस्ट्रेलिया: बेरोजगारी पाँच वर्षकै उच्च</h2><p>अस्ट्रेलियाको बेरोजगारी दर अक्टोबरमा ४.९% पुग्यो, जुन अक्टोबर २०२१ पछिकै उच्च हो। रोजगारी ४३,५०० ले घट्यो, जबकि अर्थशास्त्रीले २५,००० थपिने अपेक्षा गरेका थिए। युवा बेरोजगारी ११.३% पुग्यो। रिजर्भ बैंकले डिसेम्बर त्रैमासमा औसत ४.७% र सन् २०२७ को अन्त्यमा मात्र ४.९% पुग्ने अनुमान गरेको थियो — अक्टोबरको तथ्यांक त्योभन्दा एक वर्षभन्दा बढी अगाडि आइपुग्यो।</p><h2>जर्मनी: शरद ऋतुको नियुक्ति कमजोर</h2><p>जर्मनीमा दर्ता भएका बेरोजगार अक्टोबरमा ४०,००० ले घटेर २९ लाख ५५ हजार भए, र दर ६.३% मा झर्यो। तर मौसमी असर हटाउँदा बेरोजगारी ४,००० ले बढ्यो, र रोजगार एजेन्सी प्रमुखले आर्थिक अवस्था अझै सुधारका लागि पर्याप्त नभएको बताइन्। दर्ता भएका रिक्ति ६,५४,००० छन्। ब्रेमेनमा बेरोजगारी सबैभन्दा उच्च ११.०% र बाभारियामा सबैभन्दा न्यून ४.०% छ।</p><h2>रोजगारदाताले अब के गर्ने भन्ने योजना</h2><p>ManpowerGroup को Q4 २०२६ सर्वेक्षण अनुसार विश्वव्यापी Net Employment Outlook २९% छ — ४३% रोजगारदाताले अक्टोबर–डिसेम्बरमा कर्मचारी थप्ने योजना बनाएका छन्, १४% ले घटाउने र ४१% ले यथावत राख्ने। यो सर्वेक्षण जुलाई २०२६ मा ४२ देशका ३९,८७८ रोजगारदातासँग गरिएको हो, त्यसैले यो अगस्ट–सेप्टेम्बरका घटनाभन्दा अघिको मनसाय हो, वास्तविक नियुक्ति होइन। एसिया प्रशान्त र मध्यपूर्वमा ३३% छ, भारत सबैभन्दा उच्च ५४% छ, युएई ४१% र सिंगापुर १३% छ। सूचना क्षेत्र ४२% सहित सबैभन्दा बलियो छ।</p><h2>रिमोट काम: थोरै पद, धेरै आवेदन</h2><p>LinkedIn Economic Graph अनुसार मार्च २०२६ मा अमेरिकाका भुक्तानी गरिएका विज्ञापनमध्ये ९% पूर्ण रिमोट र ११% हाइब्रिड थिए, तर ती पूर्ण रिमोट पदमा सबै आवेदनको ३७% पर्यो। Indeed Hiring Lab अनुसार अक्टोबर २०२६ अन्त्यमा अमेरिकी विज्ञापनको ८.७% मा रिमोट वा हाइब्रिड उल्लेख थियो, जुन फेब्रुअरी २०२० मा करिब ३% थियो। बेलायतमा यो १५.९% छ।</p><h2>नोभेम्बरमा बदलिएका भिसा नियम</h2><p>अमेरिकाको H-1B शुल्क सबैभन्दा ठूलो परिवर्तन हो — र यो अहिले उठाइएको छैन। १८ सेप्टेम्बर २०२६ मा राष्ट्रपतीय घोषणापत्र २१ सेप्टेम्बर २०२७ सम्म बढाइएको छ, जसले अमेरिकाबाहिर रहेका कामदारका लागि नयाँ H-1B निवेदनमा १,००,००० डलर भुक्तानी माग्छ। तर म्यासाचुसेट्स (८ जुन २०२६) र क्यालिफोर्निया (३० सेप्टेम्बर २०२६) का दुई संघीय अदालतले यसलाई लागू गर्ने निर्देशन खारेज गरेका छन्, त्यसैले अदालतको आदेश कायम रहेसम्म शुल्क उठाउन मिल्दैन। गृह सुरक्षा विभागको छुट्टै १,०३,२६५ डलर शुल्क प्रस्ताव अझै प्रस्ताव मात्र हो।</p><h2>रोजगारी खोज्नेहरूका लागि यसको अर्थ</h2><p>पाँच देशमध्ये अमेरिका आधिकारिक तथ्यांकमा सबैभन्दा बलियो छ — रोजगारी खोज्ने प्रतिव्यक्ति सबैभन्दा धेरै खुला पद। स्वास्थ्य सेवा सबैभन्दा स्थिर क्षेत्र हो। बेलायतमा रिक्ति १२ वर्षकै न्यून छ र साना कम्पनीले पहिले नियुक्ति रोकेका छन्। क्यानडामा कमजोरी व्यापक छ, अस्ट्रेलियामा वृद्धि छिटो छ, र जर्मनीको दक्ष कामदार बाटो समग्र बजार कमजोर हुँदाहुँदै पनि खुला छ। रोजगारदाताको मनसाय भारत र खाडीमा सबैभन्दा बलियो छ।</p><h2>स्रोतले नभनेका कुरा</h2><p>नोभेम्बरको तथ्यांक अहिलेसम्म छैन: अमेरिका र क्यानडाले ४ डिसेम्बर २०२६ मा, बेलायतले १५ डिसेम्बरमा प्रतिवेदन प्रकाशित गर्छन्। यस प्रतिवेदनमा जापान, युएई वा सिंगापुरका आधिकारिक अक्टोबर तथ्यांक छैनन् — ती बजारका लागि ManpowerGroup को मनसाय मात्र छ। यस चरणमा क्यानडा, अस्ट्रेलिया वा जर्मनीका तलब तथ्यांक छैनन्। H-1B मुद्दामा सरकारले पुनरावेदन गरिरहेको छ, त्यसैले शुल्कको प्रश्न टुंगिएको छैन।</p><h2>यो प्रतिवेदन कसरी तयार भयो</h2><p>यस प्रतिवेदनका सबै तथ्यांक छेउमा नाम दिइएका आधिकारिक स्रोतबाट लिइएका हुन्, १९ नोभेम्बर २०२६ मा जाँच गरिएको। उल्लेख नगरेसम्म सबै तथ्यांक मौसमी समायोजन गरिएका हुन्। बेरोजगारी दर देशअनुसार फरक तरिकाले नापिन्छ — अमेरिका, बेलायत, क्यानडा र अस्ट्रेलियाले घरपरिवार सर्वेक्षण प्रयोग गर्छन्, जर्मनीको मुख्य दर रोजगार एजेन्सीमा दर्ता भएका बेरोजगारको गणना हो। तुलना तालिका र अमेरिकामा बेरोजगार प्रतिव्यक्ति लगभग एउटा खुला पद भन्ने गणना Growentix आफैंले उल्लेखित स्रोतबाट गरेको हो। अनुमान र पूर्वानुमान छुट्टै भनेर चिनाइएको छ।</p><p>स्रोतहरू: अमेरिकी श्रम तथ्यांक ब्यूरो, The Employment Situation — अक्टोबर २०२६ (६ नोभेम्बर २०२६) र Job Openings and Labor Turnover — सेप्टेम्बर २०२६ (३ नोभेम्बर २०२६); राष्ट्रिय तथ्यांक कार्यालय, बेलायत श्रम बजार अवलोकन: नोभेम्बर २०२६ (१७ नोभेम्बर २०२६); Statistics Canada, श्रम शक्ति सर्वेक्षण, अक्टोबर २०२६ (६ नोभेम्बर २०२६); अस्ट्रेलियाली तथ्यांक ब्यूरो, श्रम शक्ति, अस्ट्रेलिया, अक्टोबर २०२६ (१९ नोभेम्बर २०२६); संघीय रोजगार एजेन्सी, अक्टोबर २०२६ श्रम बजार तथ्यांक (३० अक्टोबर २०२६); ManpowerGroup रोजगार दृष्टिकोण सर्वेक्षण, Q4 २०२६ (८ सेप्टेम्बर २०२६); LinkedIn Economic Graph, अप्रिल २०२६ र Indeed Hiring Lab; Mintz, H-1B शुल्कसम्बन्धी राष्ट्रपतीय घोषणा (२३ सेप्टेम्बर २०२६)।</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>पाँच देशमध्ये सबैभन्दा न्यून बेरोजगारी दर कुनको छ?</h3><p>अमेरिकाको, अक्टोबर २०२६ मा ४.१%। जर्मनीको सर्वेक्षणमा आधारित दर सेप्टेम्बरमा ४.१% थियो, तर त्यो दर्ता गरिएको दर ६.३% भन्दा फरक मापन हो, त्यसैले सोझै तुलना गर्न मिल्दैन।</p><h3>के अमेरिकामा अहिले नियुक्ति भइरहेको छ?</h3><p>हो। अक्टोबर २०२६ मा रोजगारदाताले १,६५,००० रोजगारी थपे, र सेप्टेम्बर अन्त्यमा ७५ लाख खुला पद थिए — बेरोजगार प्रतिव्यक्ति लगभग एउटा। तर पूर्णकालीन काम नपाएर अंशकालीन काम गर्नेको संख्या ३,३९,००० ले बढेर ४८ लाख पुग्यो।</p><h3>ManpowerGroup सर्वेक्षणले सन् २०२७ को नियुक्तिबारे के भन्छ?</h3><p>अहिलेसम्म केही भन्दैन। सेप्टेम्बर २०२६ मा प्रकाशित सर्वेक्षणले अक्टोबर–डिसेम्बर २०२६ को मनसाय मात्र समेट्छ, जुन जुलाई २०२६ को अन्तर्वार्तामा आधारित छ। सन् २०२७ को पहिलो त्रैमास समेट्ने अर्को सर्वेक्षण डिसेम्बर २०२६ मा आउँछ।</p>$$,
